=====Usage notes=====
* Commonly elided to the clitic {{m|en|'ll}}, and {{m|en|would}} also commonly elided to {{m|en|'d}}. These are also short for {{m|en|shall}} and {{m|en|should}} (among other things).
* See also the usage notes at {{m|en|shall}}.
** Historically, {{m|en|will}} is used as a future tense auxiliary only with second and third person subjects, while {{m|en|shall}} is used with the first person. The intent sense, on the other hand, reverses this, with {{m|en|will}} being used with the first person, and {{m|en|shall}} with the second and third. This distinction may still be upheld by some speakers, especially in the UK, or in legal documents.
** Today, the person distinction is mostly lost, usually with both {{m|en|will}} and {{m|en|shall}} being used with interchangeable meaning. In particular, {{m|en|shall}} is used as a rarer or more archaic synonym of {{m|en|will}}, leaving the distinction between future and intent up to context or stress.
* Morphologically, the present tense is {{m|en|will}} and the past tense is {{m|en|would}}. In Early Modern English there was also a past participle {{m|en|would}}, but this is now obsolete.
*: Malory: ‘Many tymes he myghte haue had her and he had wold’. John Done: ‘If hee had would, hee might easily [...] occupied the Monarchy.’
* Formerly, {{m|en|go}} could be elided after {{m|en|will}} along with an adverb expressing destination, e.g. "I'll to her lodgings" (Marlowe). Compare the omission of {{m|de|gehen}} in similar situations in modern German (i.e. with an auxiliary and a destination adverb), e.g. "Ich muss in die Schule", lit. "I must in(to) the school".
* The present participle {{m|en|willing}} does not apply to the uses of {{m|en|will}} as an auxiliary verb (those senses have no participles).
* The form of {{m|en|will}} with the enclitic {{m|en|-n't}} (or the present tense negative form of {{m|en|will}} in the analysis in which {{m|en|-n't}} is an inflectional suffix) is {{m|en|won't||will not}} (rather than the form that would be expected based on a regular application of {{m|en|-n't}}, {{m|en|willn't}}), while the corresponding form of the past tense {{m|en|would}} is {{m|en|wouldn't}}.

